{"name":"Kabuki Clay","hex":"#f0dec1","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"kabuki","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A form of Japanese theatre in which elaborately costumed male performers use stylized movements, dances, and songs in order to enact tragedies and comedies.","A stylized, pretentious, and often hollow performance;  political posturing."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/kabuki"},{"term":"clay","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mineral substance made up of small crystals of silica and alumina, that is ductile when moist; the material of pre-fired ceramics.","An earth material with ductile qualities."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To add clay to or to spread clay onto.","To purify using clay."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/clay"}],"entries":[{"title":"Kabuki","blocks":[{"text":"Kabuki  is a classical form of Japanese theatre, mixing dramatic performance with traditional dance. Kabuki theatre is known for its heavily stylised performances, its glamorous, highly decorated costumes, and for the elaborate kumadori make-up worn by some of its performers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Kikugor%C5%8D_Onoe_VI_as_Ume%C5%8D-maru.jpg/330px-Kikugor%C5%8D_Onoe_VI_as_Ume%C5%8D-maru.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kabuki","wikidata":"Q199701"},{"title":"Clay","blocks":[{"text":"Clay is a type of fine-grained natural soil material containing clay minerals. Most pure clay minerals are white or light-coloured, but natural clays show a variety of colours from impurities, such as a reddish or brownish colour from small amounts of iron oxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Boulder_clay_cliff_south_of_Withernsea_%28geograph_6054962%29.jpg/330px-Boulder_clay_cliff_south_of_Withernsea_%28geograph_6054962%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm